    "memoText": "\n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 239\n\nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n\nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n\nAssembly Bill Number 7784, entitled:\n\n    \"AN  ACT to amend the state finance law, in relation to compensation\n      and medical expenses of certain injured state employees\"\n\n    NOT APPROVED\n\n  This bill would require that any  parole  officer,  parole  revocation\nspecialist, or warrant officer who is injured or taken sick, in any way,\nduring  the  performance of his or her duties be paid the full amount of\nsalary until the disability has ceased. The State would also  be  liable\nfor all hospital care necessitated by such injury or illness.\n\n  This bill as drafted, raises the same concerns as the bill I previous-\nly  vetoed two years ago. (Veto No. 516 of 2014). First, parole officers\nare already eligible for Workers' Compensation benefits  provided  under\ncurrent  law.  Any modifications to this benefit structure are therefore\nmore appropriately addressed in the  context  of  collective  bargaining\nnegotiations.\n\n  Second,  it is estimated that this bill would cost the State nearly $4\nmillion in increased Workers'  Compensation  and  overtime  payments  to\ncover  lost  hours. This cost is not accounted for in the State's fiscal\nplan, and thus would ultimately be borne by the State's  taxpayers.  For\nthese reasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n\n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n\n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 239,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2016
   }
